# ECP extensions and plugins

Treat every extension as if it were published for third-party authors:

- Environment-agnostic.
- Minimal dependencies.
- No imports from host packages.

The contract is changing during the reset. "Today" is what the code does now; "Target" is what
the phase docs build. Don't add new uses of anything marked as going away.

## Dependencies

| Package | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `@executioncontrolprotocol/core` and `@executioncontrolprotocol/core/types` | `defineExtension`, `capabilityFor`, `definePolicy`, protocol types, validation, file I/O (`resolveFile`, `writeMediaArtifact`) |
| `zod` | Config and capability I/O schemas. Import from `"zod"` directly |
| Focused third-party libraries | Only what the extension's job needs |
| `@executioncontrolprotocol/plugin-sdk` (from Phase 3) | Generated CLI commands. Never hand-written oclif code |

Never depend on `@executioncontrolprotocol/node`, `browser`, `cli` or `mcp`.

## Definition

```ts
import { defineExtension, capabilityFor } from "@executioncontrolprotocol/core"
import { z } from "zod"

export default defineExtension("@vendor", "my-ext")
  .withConfig({ apiKey: z.string().optional() })
  .withMetadata({ summary, description, useCases })
  .withCapabilities([
    capabilityFor("@vendor/my-ext", "do-thing")
      .withInput(z.object({ value: z.string() }))
      .withOutput(z.object({ result: z.string() }))
      .withMetadata({ summary, description, useCases, projections })
      .withHandler(async (input) => ({ result: input.value })),
  ])
  .build()
```

## What changes, and when

| Concern | Today | Target |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Registration | Export the definition; bind `extension(def)` on the environment. Hosts call `registry.registerExtension(definition)` (no `register*` helpers) | Unchanged after Phase 5. `ecp plugins build` generates CLI files |
| Where it can run | `.supports("@executioncontrolprotocol/node", "^1")`. No call means any environment | Unchanged after Phase 2 |
| Package name | Same as the extension id (`@executioncontrolprotocol/format-toon`) | **Phase 5:** `@<scope>/plugin-<extension name>` (`plugin-format-toon`). Ids don't change |
| Location | First-party plugins live in `plugins/plugin-*` | Unchanged after Phase 5 PR 4 |
| CLI | None | **Phase 3/5:** `ecp plugins build` generates commands, manifest and `package.json` fields. Nothing hand-written |
| Policies | `plugin-budget`, `plugin-approval`, `plugin-state-control`; `registry-control` is in core | Unchanged after Phase 5 PR 2 |
| Secrets providers | Node environment default bindings (`secrets`, `process-env`) | Unchanged after Phase 2. `browser-secrets` was removed in Phase 1 |

## Metadata (`.withMetadata`)

Write agent-facing prose once; the CLI help, MCP tool descriptions and `ecp describe` all
read it.

| Do | Don't |
| --- | --- |
| Write product prose: behaviour, when to use it, limits | Paraphrase schema fields or say "read inputSchema" |
| Give at least one `useCases` entry (when to call) and one `projections` entry (what to take from the output) | Sample prompts, sample payloads, path syntax; omitting projections |
| Name sibling capabilities inside the same extension when useful | Cross-advertise other packages ("prefer ollama over …") |
| For transform and parse capabilities, explain how to reduce bulky output to workflow-sized facts | Only say "take the result" |

Hosts stay vendor-neutral. They read projections as plain text and never branch on capability
ids or output shapes.

## Handlers and I/O

- Handlers receive typed input and a context from core. Don't reach into `Environment`
  internals.
- Use `capabilityFor("@vendor/name", "capability-id")` for stable ids.
- For binary data, use `resolveFile` / `writeMediaArtifact`. Don't reimplement fs, fetch or
  artifact maps.
- **Node-only code** (native modules, `node:fs`, Node SDKs): declare it with
  `.supports("@executioncontrolprotocol/node")`. Keep the package root importable in a
  browser, using a `browser` export condition where needed.
- **Format capabilities** (`encode`/`decode`):
  - Accept `unknown` documents and infer `schema` when present.
  - Validate known schemas with diagnostics.
  - Only throw on unknown schemas in `strict` mode.
- **Bound config** must satisfy the definition's `withConfig` schema. Phase 4 validates it at
  load time.

## Tests

- Test the extension on its own, with fixtures.
- Use `environment()` from core for encode and decode tests; don't pull in a host to call
  `describe()` or `run()`.
- In-repo stub: `testExtension` from `@executioncontrolprotocol/core`, only when a real
  handler must run.
- From Phase 5, every plugin also has one CLI-level test of a generated command with `--json`,
  and passes `ecp plugins check`.
